<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardèche</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ardèche is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, named after the river Ardèche, known for its gorges. It largely corresponds to the old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vivarais">Vivarais</a>, the land of the Gallic Helvii in antiquity. The Chauvet Cave, with some of the oldest known cave paintings, lies within it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardeche.jpg" alt="Ardèche" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Hager · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont_d%27Arc_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> covers 5,550 square kilometres and is a land of great contrasts. It lies between 40 metres above sea level, where the Ardèche river flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, and 1,754 metres at Mont Mézenc. The Rhône valley borders it on the east for 140 km and the high plateaus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> on the west. Its prefecture, Privas, is 140 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and the department's mountainous relief makes travel between its parts difficult, especially in winter. It has five natural regions: the western mountains, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> has its source, the plateaus of the Haut-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vivarais">Vivarais</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cevennes">Cévennes</a>, the limestone Bas-Vivarais with its almost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, the basalt plateau of the Coirons and the Rhône valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans have lived in the area since at least the Upper Paleolithic, as the cave paintings at Chauvet Pont d'Arc attest, and dolmens and menhirs stand on the river's plateau. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vivarais">Vivarais</a> takes its name from Viviers, capital of the Gaulish Helvii, and Auxonius moved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat there in 430. Raids by Hungarians and Saracen slavers in the 9th century depopulated the region. It was part of the Kingdom of Burgundy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, formed in 933, and was left to the emperor by its last king in 1032; it passed to the French crown in 1229, and by the treaty of 10 July 1305 Philip IV obliged the bishops of Vivarais to admit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the French king.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vivarais">Vivarais</a> became a Protestant stronghold after Calvin's reformation, with over 75 Protestant churches by 1598 and eight pitched battles between 1562 and 1595. In 1629 Privas was burned to the ground by the forces of Louis XIII, and one fifth of the Protestant population emigrated. The Revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685 led to the Camisard revolt and to dragonnades by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s dragoons; peace came only in 1715, and another 50,000 Protestants fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On 4 June 1783 the Montgolfier brothers, sons of a paper-maker, made the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hot-air-balloon">hot air balloon</a> ascent over Annonay.</p><p>The Ardèche was one of the 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created on 4 March 1790. Its population rose from 273,000 in 1793 to 388,500 in 1861, a peak not since matched, while the silkworm industry collapsed after disease struck in 1855. The last blast furnace closed at Pouzain in 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> has 334,231 inhabitants, called Ardéchois. Its prefecture is Privas, and its largest city is Annonay. Rivers and gorges draw canoeists and kayakers, and the department holds part of the Cévennes National Park. The Ardèche chestnut, the châtaigne d'Ardèche, received appellation d'origine contrôlée status in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ardèche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Helvii lived on the right bank of the Rhône in the modern <b>Ardèche</b>, at the edge of the Massif Central.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvii">Helvii</a></li></ul></section>
